+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Dot Product & Matrix Multiply</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The <strong>dot product</strong> is the most fundamental operation in linear algebra. For two 1-D arrays, it computes the sum of element-wise products. For 2-D arrays, it becomes <strong>matrix multiplication</strong> \u2014 the operation that drives every neural network layer, every PCA projection, and every linear transformation.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Dot product \u2014 sum of element-wise products of two vectors:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i b_i = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\|\\,\\|\\mathbf{b}\\|\\cos\\theta$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">a \u00b7 b</div><div class="card-body">Dot product (scalar) of vectors a and b. Two equivalent definitions: algebraic (sum of products) and geometric (magnitudes times cosine of angle).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">a_i, b_i</div><div class="card-body">i-th components of the vectors. Both vectors must have the same dimension n.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">\u2016a\u2016, \u2016b\u2016</div><div class="card-body">Magnitudes (lengths) of the vectors. Always positive.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">cos \u03b8</div><div class="card-body">Cosine of the angle between the two vectors. cos 0\u00b0 = 1 (parallel, max dot product), cos 90\u00b0 = 0 (orthogonal), cos 180\u00b0 = \u22121 (antiparallel).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Why it matters</div><div class="card-body">Foundation of similarity, projection, and neural network layers (Wx + b). Every attention score in a Transformer is a dot product.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Matrix multiplication \u2014 entry-wise definition:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$C_{ij} = \\sum_{k=1}^{p} A_{ik} B_{kj}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">C_{ij}</div><div class="card-body">Element at row i, column j of the result matrix C = AB.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">A_{ik}</div><div class="card-body">Element at row i, column k of A. As k varies, this sweeps across row i of A.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">B_{kj}</div><div class="card-body">Element at row k, column j of B. As k varies, this sweeps down column j of B.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">\u03a3 over k</div><div class="card-body">Sum runs along the shared/inner dimension p. C_{ij} is the dot product of row i of A and column j of B.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Shape rule</div><div class="card-body">A is (m \u00d7 p), B is (p \u00d7 n), result C is (m \u00d7 n). Inner p dimensions must match; outer m \u00d7 n becomes the result shape.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.dot(a, b)</div><div class="card-body">General-purpose: 1-D dot product, 2-D matrix multiply, N-D sum over last/second-to-last axes. The Swiss Army knife.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">a @ b</div><div class="card-body">Matrix multiplication operator (Python 3.5+). Cleaner syntax, same result as np.matmul. Preferred for readability.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.matmul</div><div class="card-body">Strict matrix multiply. Does NOT broadcast scalars. Supports batch multiplication for 3-D+ arrays (stacks of matrices).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">inner / outer</div><div class="card-body">np.inner = dot product along last axis. np.outer = rank-1 outer product, producing an (n,m) matrix from vectors of length n and m.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># === 1-D Dot Product ===</span>\na = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>])\nb = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>])\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">dot</span>(a, b))     <span class="cm"># 32  (1*4 + 2*5 + 3*6)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(a @ b)             <span class="cm"># 32  (same thing)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">inner</span>(a, b))   <span class="cm"># 32  (same for 1-D)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># === Matrix Multiplication (2-D) ===</span>\nA = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>],\n              [<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>]])   <span class="cm"># (2, 2)</span>\nB = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>],\n              [<span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">8</span>]])   <span class="cm"># (2, 2)</span>\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(A @ B)\n<span class="cm"># [[19 22]     1*5+2*7  1*6+2*8</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [43 50]]    3*5+4*7  3*6+4*8</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># === Shape Rules ===</span>\nC = np.<span class="fn">ones</span>((<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>))   <span class="cm"># (3, 4)</span>\nD = np.<span class="fn">ones</span>((<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>))   <span class="cm"># (4, 5)</span>\nresult = C @ D           <span class="cm"># (3, 5)  inner dims must match!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># === Outer Product ===</span>\nu = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>])\nv = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">outer</span>(u, v))\n<span class="cm"># [[10 20]</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [20 40]</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [30 60]]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Shape compatibility:</strong> For <code>A @ B</code>, the number of <em>columns</em> of A must equal the number of <em>rows</em> of B. The result shape is (rows of A, columns of B). This is the single most important rule in matrix multiplication.</p>'

