+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> 50 noisy data points generated from y = 3x + 5. The red line is the least-squares fit computed by <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code>. It finds the slope and intercept that minimize the total squared distance from all points to the line.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Never use <code>inv</code> when you can use <code>solve</code>:</strong> Computing A\u207b\u00b9 explicitly takes O(n\u00b3) operations and doubles rounding errors. <code>np.linalg.solve</code> uses LU decomposition internally and is both faster and more numerically stable. Reserve <code>inv</code> for when you genuinely need the inverse matrix (rare in practice).</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: Eigenvalues & SVD
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Eigenvalues & SVD</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Eigenvalues and eigenvectors</strong> reveal a matrix\'s fundamental behavior: they tell you the directions in which a linear transformation simply <em>stretches</em> without rotating. <strong>Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)</strong> generalizes this to any matrix (not just square ones) and is the backbone of PCA, recommendation systems, and image compression.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Eigenvalue equation — the heart of spectral analysis:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">A</div><div class="card-body">A square (n × n) matrix representing a linear transformation.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">v</div><div class="card-body">Eigenvector — a non-zero vector whose direction is preserved (or flipped) by A. The transformation only stretches it.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">λ (lambda)</div><div class="card-body">Eigenvalue — the scalar stretch factor. λ = 2 means A doubles v\'s length; λ = −1 flips its direction; λ = 0 collapses it to zero.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.linalg.eig</div><div class="card-body">Returns all eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A. For symmetric matrices use np.linalg.eigh which is faster and numerically more stable.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">ML use</div><div class="card-body">PCA finds eigenvectors of the covariance matrix; the largest eigenvalues reveal the directions of maximum variance.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Singular Value Decomposition — works for ANY matrix:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$A = U \\Sigma V^{T}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">A</div><div class="card-body">Any matrix of shape (m × n). Unlike eigendecomposition, SVD does NOT require A to be square.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">U</div><div class="card-body">(m × m) orthogonal matrix. Its columns are the left singular vectors — basis for the column space.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Σ (Sigma)</div><div class="card-body">(m × n) diagonal matrix of non-negative singular values, sorted in descending order. They measure how much each direction is stretched.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">V^T</div><div class="card-body">(n × n) transpose of an orthogonal matrix V. Rows of V^T (columns of V) are the right singular vectors — basis for the row space.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Truncated SVD</div><div class="card-body">Keeping only the top-k singular values gives the best rank-k approximation of A. This is the basis of dimensionality reduction, image compression, and LSA.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.linalg.eig</div><div class="card-body">Eigendecomposition for general square matrices. Returns (eigenvalues, eigenvectors). For symmetric/Hermitian matrices, use np.linalg.eigh (faster, numerically better).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.linalg.svd</div><div class="card-body">Decomposes A = U \u03a3 V\u1d40. Works on ANY matrix (m\u00d7n). U = left singular vectors, \u03a3 = singular values, V\u1d40 = right singular vectors.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">PCA via SVD</div><div class="card-body">Center data, compute SVD, project onto top-k right singular vectors. Singular values = sqrt(eigenvalues of covariance). This IS how sklearn does PCA.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># === Eigendecomposition ===</span>\nA = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">2</span>],\n              [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">3</span>]])\n\neigenvalues, eigenvectors = np.linalg.<span class="fn">eig</span>(A)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Eigenvalues:"</span>, eigenvalues)    <span class="cm"># [5. 2.]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Eigenvectors:\\n"</span>, eigenvectors)\n<span class="cm"># Each COLUMN is an eigenvector</span>\n<span class="cm"># Av = \u03bbv  \u2192  A @ v[:,0] == eigenvalues[0] * v[:,0]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Verify: Av = \u03bbv</span>\nv0 = eigenvectors[:, <span class="num">0</span>]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">allclose</span>(A @ v0, eigenvalues[<span class="num">0</span>] * v0))  <span class="cm"># True</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># For symmetric matrices, use eigh (guaranteed real eigenvalues)</span>\nSym = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">2</span>],\n                [<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>]])\nvals, vecs = np.linalg.<span class="fn">eigh</span>(Sym)  <span class="cm"># sorted ascending!</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(vals)  <span class="cm"># [1.76, 6.24] (always real for symmetric)</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># === Singular Value Decomposition ===</span>\nA = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>],\n              [<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>]])\n\nU, sigma, Vt = np.linalg.<span class="fn">svd</span>(A, full_matrices=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"U shape: {U.shape}"</span>)      <span class="cm"># (2, 2)</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"sigma: {sigma}"</span>)          <span class="cm"># [9.508, 0.773]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Vt shape: {Vt.shape}"</span>)    <span class="cm"># (2, 3)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Reconstruct: A = U @ diag(sigma) @ Vt</span>\nA_reconstructed = U @ np.<span class="fn">diag</span>(sigma) @ Vt\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">allclose</span>(A, A_reconstructed))  <span class="cm"># True</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># === PCA from Scratch ===</span>\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\n<span class="cm"># Correlated 2D data</span>\ndata = np.random.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="num">200</span>, <span class="num">2</span>) @ np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">1</span>], [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>]])\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 1: Center the data</span>\ndata_centered = data - data.<span class="fn">mean</span>(axis=<span class="num">0</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 2: SVD</span>\nU, S, Vt = np.linalg.<span class="fn">svd</span>(data_centered, full_matrices=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 3: Variance explained</span>\nvariance_explained = (S ** <span class="num">2</span>) / (S ** <span class="num">2</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Variance explained:"</span>, variance_explained)\n<span class="cm"># e.g. [0.87, 0.13] \u2192 PC1 explains 87% of variance</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Step 4: Project to 1D</span>\ndata_1d = data_centered @ Vt[<span class="num">0</span>]  <span class="cm"># project onto first PC</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">For higher-dimensional data, SVD-based PCA scales efficiently. Here is PCA on a 5-feature dataset:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># PCA on higher-dimensional data</span>\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">0</span>)\nX = np.random.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="num">300</span>, <span class="num">5</span>) @ np.<span class="fn">diag</span>([<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">0.5</span>, <span class="num">0.1</span>])\nX_centered = X - X.<span class="fn">mean</span>(axis=<span class="num">0</span>)\n\nU, S, Vt = np.linalg.<span class="fn">svd</span>(X_centered, full_matrices=<span class="kw">False</span>)\nvar_explained = (S**<span class="num">2</span>) / (S**<span class="num">2</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>()\ncum_var = np.<span class="fn">cumsum</span>(var_explained)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Individual:"</span>, np.<span class="fn">round</span>(var_explained, <span class="num">3</span>))\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Cumulative:"</span>, np.<span class="fn">round</span>(cum_var, <span class="num">3</span>))\n<span class="cm"># Individual: [0.714, 0.257, 0.026, 0.003, 0.000]</span>\n<span class="cm"># Cumulative: [0.714, 0.971, 0.997, 1.000, 1.000]</span>\n<span class="cm"># \u2192 2 components capture 97% of variance!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Project to 2D for visualization</span>\nX_2d = X_centered @ Vt[:<span class="num">2</span>].T  <span class="cm"># (300, 2)</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: PCA Scree Plot (EN) --- */
+ '<div id="plot-pca-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ 'var comps=["PC1","PC2","PC3","PC4","PC5"];'
+ 'var indiv=[71.4,25.7,2.6,0.3,0.02];'
